as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/606b8a3c79391a14d997ef7231295e1e.png)" 13194139535688 false] [17592186166469 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535688 true] [17592186166469 1100 "Consider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/788240fc680483e8d3c84baf05680420.png)\n\n1. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n2. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n3. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\n4. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$" 13194139535688 false] [17592186166469 1145 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/343f6f32ac21a1bf06b86174734dd9ebba6ddb7598cbe83b4ade59f70e8e8b26.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/cb0f32e55af289452f1429ddfe3214a46501c55f3a61deaf10b4f736e47dca7f.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85fdb4fc83a6f2b44178be2e97a0e7985d97350deb4e0fb123e513a80090906a.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3f984a493e976bd522455395defcb4c1562d61ca56e19fa27e2272832ce6011c.png)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/44f82894493c95c38039bc1e6a842bb7226571e9818e3c10c50d33627464ea37.png)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535688 true] [17592186166469 1145 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0$.\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0$.\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)\n$$" 13194139535688 false] [17592186166472 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." 13194139535688 true] [17592186166472 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 4y(t)$\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." 13194139535688 false] [17592186166472 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7f53fdda091242e4fee1edd70a7e96c649eff8bac93fcdc9fed6da38ec5c7523.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3b9909cba711a2394b8dbcda4b9d72ffbd9d37bd24b90506ac22b44c4cd398c4.png)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/1ea46da3e9697ee89d1112dfec53457657271c391a28a6c55267da70d38157c4.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9725d696549426d16093b7cd0e9bb188589472ed9ff81520c19a07ab296fb96d.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85e8d745e2b504770815bb92ad1a151f422c941874f33840593836e78eeaacde.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2f67b02cda725daa0d0b994f3f4952065793e53ddc32260b14310f5158876f4f.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186166472 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}$.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/fbc80d18c07900cef20d4276d4e2c539.png)" 13194139535688 false] [17592186166474 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186166474 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) =-4y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/5b9c9d82e7a3509295ac890d9c2e3e29.png)" 13194139535688 false] [17592186166474 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/703fb7adc0ca076e42aecc781d4d0dc58eb5f4fec03db4d18bf5a36f7055a349.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7210186106fc202e3b9a35ffa32b06b09493ce28c191a89c2824a35a547febff.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/f292220b15ff9b0d695cb8b9f2349cc275557fd2b77c32c147e25365ab0f7e47.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/5671487738e750f488ad61852f17fb4b6b14cf4050fee66338000d4939914ce6.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/651cc55c8b96c402fc00140c06736493e71276666ce4eb1ba74c2294600a1b89.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/222dcd944fc89cd0f07970cddf5c50aa167c7f2d25b1f7305779c3dbb0a4a6af.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/08ad54b92f62513fb88f12f7bd6da358263e46673d686e0982a1e8044b8ab131.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186166474 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/808f8621a6a3c0729a4f09a90e506461.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_1e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t)=c_2e^{-4t}\n$$\n\nprovided that\n\n$$\nc_1,c_2 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t)) = ((c_{2}e^{-4t})^{′})/((c_{1}e^{-t})^{′}) = \\frac{-4c_{2}e^{-4t}}{-c_{1}e^{-t}} = \\frac{4c_{2}}{c_{1}}e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source