{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361302 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361303 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361304 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361305 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361306 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1041 "selection" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361304 13194139535688 true] [17592186361301 1110 #uuid "72d93df0-3cf3-5882-90d6-60a03b1d0cba" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361302 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535688 true] [17592186361302 1111 #uuid "be342178-890e-51a4-aec7-763396e084c9" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361303 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535688 true] [17592186361303 1111 #uuid "4186a912-08f8-5e3a-a5ee-9f1d19e10209" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361304 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535688 true] [17592186361304 1111 #uuid "03c96236-6e1e-51a3-99e9-1aba09ee3ddf" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361305 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535688 true] [17592186361305 1111 #uuid "9c371ebc-0600-5990-8052-c1beb76158cc" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361306 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535688 true] [17592186361306 1111 #uuid "1bb44816-ac9f-50fb-94bc-5d0edc245bda" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361308 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1089 "q-332751" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1113 #uuid "7a71e1e9-c69e-580f-aa68-2f882f086377" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361307 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361309 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361310 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361311 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361312 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361313 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1041 "selection" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361311 13194139535688 true] [17592186361