186361315 13194139535688 true] [17592186361314 1089 "q-333177" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361314 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535688 true] [17592186361314 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361314 1113 #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361314 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361316 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361317 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361318 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361319 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361320 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1041 "selection" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361319 13194139535688 true] [17592186361315 1110 #uuid "4e77d1cb-3c2e-5b2b-9c22-2b27c0142fbe" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361316 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/4f613e414cdb619a9b09f25a47b7e4cad953440c40d91f876322d7e1cb59b8c4.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361316 1111 #uuid "a584ae8b-b1c8-50fa-8f02-c29ebdd9d58b" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361317 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/635e8687fbccc7612f692ec1a1abaa9802bafe9c3773e4b0c058a18813e7249e.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361317 1111 #uuid "11780fdc-41ab-5191-968b-7446ccada44f" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361318 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a5ae215022638430d0c89dddd97c01ae7598b6251bd013ef48ca9771029b8cd6.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361318 1111 #uuid "148d8f21-24e1-51d6-ad23-a847e1a1806a" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361319 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361319 1111 #uuid "2f2dc63f-0cd3-55d5-bdcb-27d2ac53c614" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361320 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c9816edf7fb99b759361abc4333f3ed0d3676d2c77db2210367466b66837bca8.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361320 1111 #uuid "358cd386-9b70-5d8e-9f37-7721182d21e4" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361322 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1089 "q-333184" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1113 #uuid "91fc904e-97e1-5e34-b331-513c29eb83a1" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361321 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361323 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361324 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361325 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361326 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361327 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1041 "selection" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361325 13194139535688 true] [17592186361322 1110 #uuid "e0a10bce-add0-5880-beac-b7920e570aac" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361323 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361323 1111 #uuid "e18a48f2-a3e6-5993-bd4d-8bc1544e7dbb" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361324 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361324 1111 #uuid "78b65467-beeb-50a9-9d8e-90f072a5b5ff" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361325 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361325 1111 #uuid "818b06b6-bdb5-511f-bf8d-ef51c4130f09" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361326 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361326 1111 #uuid "8501b0f6-47e4-54bb-bb05-3c822f5a0bde" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361327 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361327 1111 #uuid "54d88cae-6632-5589-9054-d0e2e12ff77b" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361329 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1089 "q-333192" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1113 #uuid "5c54e4f5-9ca4-5f84-ad60-e84341fc1bfd" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361328 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b1efa82f75d22a304e29adc5a791086bb9d9bf43b2f5c97ba8563f43fd13cc4a.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361330 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361331 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361332 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361333 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361334 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1041 "selection" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361331 13194139535688 true] [17592186361329 1110 #uuid "5d84b924-2262-5e1f-a86a-a051161a7c6e" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361330 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361330 1111 #uuid "ea7c55b8-3874-5d56-a05a-b32c7c1fbfdf" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361331 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361331 1111 #uuid "995de0c4-f622-5377-ae9f-ea063bea83b8" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361332 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b829471223fd7bc2bd7a6c1183729f6eda34260822edc7c9cb8b6e86a7a55e6e.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361332 1111 #uuid "277a5490-6f2d-56bb-a279-e29f7399eb66" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361333 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e7a9385b508c7bb1591501fd8bc830faa78ebc3a2f2ad8279ecfe88328ce8bbd.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361333 1111 #uuid "f02986ff-c6f1-5c25-9d12-71e202f215e6" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361334 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/71c58730ea546deee365729cab6f1cae3f9aa73409a302067dbbf91fdd85a24d.png" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535688 true] [17592186361334 1111 #uuid "e089de5e-fe0b-53ab-8c53-e682261f6d74" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361336 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361339 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361342 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1089 "q-333213" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1113 #uuid "d329e132-aa2f-50d8-8bad-87983411294d" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361335 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)" 13194139535688 true] [17592186361336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361337 13194139535688 true] [17592186361336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361338 13194139535688 true] [17592186361336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535